23
00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:16.233
An input guard can check your user's input
or any retrieved text

24
00:01:16.366 --> 00:01:20.233
before they are passed to the LLM.
And an output guard checks

25
00:01:20.233 --> 00:01:24.233
any response returned by the LLM
against your validation rules.

26
00:01:24.833 --> 00:01:28.833
As you begin implementing guardrails,
you'll need to know some terminology.

27
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:32.266
The validator is the

28
00:01:32.266 --> 00:01:37.266
core piece of logic that lies at the heart
of a guardrail, and implements the code

29
00:01:37.566 --> 00:01:42.433
to check that inputs or outputs
conform to your specific validation rules.

30
00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:45.800
You'll often hear me
call this a guardrail.

31
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:49.333
A guard, on the other hand,
is part of your application stack,

32
00:01:49.566 --> 00:01:53.933
and it handles the processing of inputs
outputs to pass to the validator.

33
00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:57.700
A guard can actually contain
more than one guardrail.

34
00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.533
And in this lesson,
you'll use the validator and guard classes

35
00:02:01.533 --> 00:02:04.533
to implement
a simple guard for your RAG chatbot.

36
00:02:04.966 --> 00:02:08.700
So coming back to the notebook,
you'll import some classes

37
00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:11.866
and functions from the Guardrail's
AI Python SDK.

38
00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:17.666
First of all, we're going to import the
guard on fail action and settings class.

39
00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:19.733
The guard is really straightforward.

40
00:02:19.733 --> 00:02:21.700
It is basically a container

41
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.700
for many different guardrails,
so that you can mix and match

42
00:02:24.766 --> 00:02:27.933
